# knobs_9x9.env -- mission ktg-train, node arxiv-1902.10565::derive_cycle_knobs_9x9.
# The production cycle knobs, and the single source of truth for them.
# Obligations o24_cycle_knobs_derived and the knob conjunct of o13_loop_config_paths.
#
# Sourceable: `set -a; . knobs_9x9.env; set +a` exports every knob into the
# ${VAR:-default} block of codes/loop/synchronous_loop_9x9.sh (CHANGE 9), whose
# defaults are identical to the values here (asserted by
# `codes/eval/derive_knobs.py --assert-loop-defaults`).
#
# NOTHING HERE IS HAND-PICKED (DESIGN section 8 R14). Every value is the solution of a
# constraint read out of the reference code at path:line, evaluated at a number some job
# actually measured. The set was first derived from Slurm jobs 298712 / 299259 (the loop
# smoke); NUM_GAMES_PER_CYCLE was RE-DERIVED on 2026-09-05 (a) from 41 production cycles of
# job 301099, 1000 -> 1500, and (b) from 61 cycles of the same job, 1500 -> 1800, together
# with NUM_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_EPOCH 20000 -> 16000 and its two dependants -- the OPTION C of
# evidence/scale_data_window/o47_options.md, chosen by the human (mission.json decisions,
# 2026-09-05, "link 2 onward runs OPTION C"). All three sets of inputs are recorded below.
# Smoke numbers are read from the
# job-suffixed evidence copies
# evidence/smoke/{rows_per_game,throughput_smoke,nlwp_max,audit}-298712/299259.* -- never
# from the unsuffixed names, which attempt 2 overwrote (obligation o37).
# Re-check the CURRENT set against the PRODUCTION measurement with:
#   python3 codes/eval/check_knobs_9x9.py \
#     --throughput evidence/production_chain/throughput.json \
#     --rows-file  evidence/production_chain/rows_per_game.txt \
#     --marginal-nets 3 --trend-nets 9 --horizon-nets 10
# and against the frozen smoke evidence with the parameter-free
#   python3 codes/eval/check_knobs_9x9.py
# The underlying arithmetic, at either set of inputs:
#   python3 codes/eval/derive_knobs.py \
#     --rows-per-game 17.1736 --rows-per-game-lower 15.4318 \
#     --reuse 8 --samples-per-epoch 16000 --games 1800 --keep 120000 --cap 80000 \
#     --min-rows 25000 --taper 50000 --batch 128 --cpus 32 --game-threads 18
#
# --gate-games-measured is needed only for the two -298712 / -299259 files, which were
# written before audit_smoke.py emitted `gatekeeper_games_total` (156 is that job's
# measured gatekeeper sgf line count, audit-298712.json S4_gatekeeper_sgfs.lines, and
# check_knobs_9x9.py reads it from there rather than taking it as an argument). Obligation
# o41: derive_knobs.py has no fallback for any measured key -- a missing one is an exit
# naming the key, never a constant.
#
# MEASURED INPUTS -- jobs 298712 (attempt 1) and 299259 (attempt 2, ppid-filtered sampler,
# no foreign pids). Cited by name: evidence/smoke/audit-298712.json and audit-299259.json;
# sha256 of every file read is in evidence/derive_cycle_knobs/derivation.md section 1.
#   r      = 32.3    rows/game, REAL net   (646 rows / 20 games, 298712 leg-D1 probe)
#                    corroborated 31.95 (639/20) and 34.4 (2064/60) by 299259's two probes;
#                    all three re-run in derivation.txt block 4 and none moves a knob
#   r_lo   = 25.0775 = r * (1 - 1/sqrt(20)), the section 11 lower bound used for every
#            binding inequality, so a real r below the 20-game measurement still holds
#   r0     = 31.675  rows/game, random net (2534 rows / 80 games)
#   r0_lo  = 28.1336 = r0 * (1 - 1/sqrt(80))
#   353.8  bytes/row on disk (896536 B / 2534 rows) -- SUPERSEDED by the production
#          366.27 B/row (350347842 B / 956527 rows); the smoke figure was 3.5 % optimistic
#          and every storage line below is now at 366.27. As measured it was 10.94 KiB/game at r0 and
#          11.16 KiB/game at r -- both above the 10 KiB/game c10 second conjunct, which
#          299259 refuted; the guard arithmetic below uses B/row, never KiB/game
#   2031.7 real-net selfplay games/h (299259, 80 real-net games / 141.75 s); 298712's
#          20-game probe gave 2341.5, and check_knobs_9x9.py takes the SLOWER of the two
#   14.243 train samples/s all-in (298712; torch.compile included); 299259 gave 17.202
#          and the check again takes the slower
#   threads: 22 random-net selfplay, 25 any CUDA-context stage, 14 train, 4+8 shuffle --
#          298712 and 299259 agree, and 299259's sampler had no foreign pids at all
#
# NOT USED, deliberately: nothing here is derived from cheapSearchProb or from assumption
# a07_moves_per_game_80. 299259 measured full_frac = 0.342 +- 0.006 over three runs against
# the 1 - cheapSearchProb = 0.25 the derivation.md section 4 arithmetic predicts, so the
# "(1-0.75)*80 + 0.02*80 ~ 22 rows/game" chain is refuted. Every row count below is the
# measured rows/game, which is what makes this derivation independent of that failure.

# --- the eleven loop-block knobs -------------------------------------------------

# RE-DERIVED TWICE ON 2026-09-05, 1000 -> 1500 -> 1800. The 1000 rested on r = 32.3
# rows/game, a 20-game probe against the smoke's barely-trained net; rows/game falls as the
# net learns to stop filling the board, and production refutes any single r as a constant of
# the run. Moves/game collapsed 130.8 -> ~81 between the first and third accepted net and
# has sat at 80-82 since (a one-time transition, over), while rows/move drifts 0.252 ->
# 0.227 on top of it. Resignations are 0.0 % in EVERY net directory, so the shortening is
# not resignation. The two moves:
#   1000 -> 1500 at status_log.txt read 4 (41 cycles): marginal r = 18.525 over the last
#            three complete net dirs, and T1 was VIOLATED at 1000 games -- a cycle no longer
#            produced even ONE epoch's worth of new rows (18525 < E = 20000).
#   1500 -> 1800 at reads 5-6 (61 and 65 cycles), together with E 20000 -> 16000:
#            obligation o47_rows_per_game_drift_rederive_at_17 and the human's OPTION C
#            decision. The 1500 file was never consumed by a link -- it was staged at read 5
#            and superseded at read 6, before the link-1 -> link-2 boundary.
#
# G is the solution of the same inequalities, at the MARGINAL r -- the rate the NEXT cycle
# actually gets, the whole-run aggregate being flattered by the early long-game nets. At the
# read-5 marginal 17.7235 and its 10-net bound 16.6532, where option C was derived:
#   freshness (T1)   G >= 1.2*E / r     = 19200 / 17.7235 = 1083.3
#                    G >= 1.2*E / r_lo  = 19200 / 16.6532 = 1152.9
#   bootstrap (K5)   G >= min_rows / r0_lo = 25000 / 31.028 = 805.7
#   reuse            G >= 1.2 * (1.2*E_old) / r_lo = 28800 / 16.6532 = 1729.4   <- binding
# The binding line is NOT T1. It is the requirement that the reuse the loop REALLY applies
# stay at the value it had before the drift -- stated against the OLD epoch 20000, so the
# bound is a statement about data per cycle and not about how much easier the test becomes
# when E falls. Measured (evidence/scale_data_window/
# epoch_granularity.txt): the shuffle writes the kept sample as round(keep/70000) = 2 output
# files -- 60136 + 59864 = 120000 rows exactly -- and train.py:1306-1345 takes WHOLE files,
# because its probabilistic skip at :1332 needs batches_to_use_so_far > 0 and a first file
# of 467 batches never allows it. An epoch therefore trains one whole file (measured global
# steps 6557440 -> 6617216 -> 6677248 = 467 and 469 batches, against the nominal
# round(E/128)), and the third epoch of each cycle exits 0 on "Not enough data files to fill
# a subepoch" (174 epochs started, 62 aborted there, 22 exports in 62 cycles). So
#   samples per cycle  = SHUFFLE_KEEPROWS,  not epochs * E
#   real reuse   rho   = SHUFFLE_KEEPROWS / (G * r)
# Requiring rho <= 120000/28800 = 4.167 is that "T1 with a 20 % margin" line, and 1729.4
# rounds up to 1800.
#
# AT THE FRESHEST MEASUREMENT (read 6, 65 cycles, 2026-09-05T06:17) THE DRIFT HAS STEEPENED:
# marginal 17.1736, slope -0.1742 (1.6x the read-5 -0.1070), 10-net bound 15.4318. This set
# still holds there, and the steepening is what retires the staged 1500 file:
#   G=1500 E=20000  rows/cycle 25760.4 vs 24000  +7.33 %;  at r_lo 23147.7  -3.55 %  FAILS
#   G=1800 E=16000  rows/cycle 30912.5 vs 19200 +61.00 %;  at r_lo 27777.2 +44.67 %  holds
# rho at read 6: 6.99 at link 1's G=1000 (87 % of the MAX_TRAIN_PER_DATA cap of 8, and the
# mechanism behind the measured value-loss drift), 4.66 at G=1500, 3.88 at G=1800.
# HONEST DECOMPOSITION of the +61.00 %: raising G moves the NUMERATOR (-28.44 % -> +28.80 %
# against the unchanged 24000 threshold) and lowering E moves the THRESHOLD (24000 ->
# 19200, +28.80 % -> +61.00 %). About half the headline gain is the test getting easier,
# not the run getting more data; the E change is justified by the SWA horizon and the
# metrics floor, not by T1. Only the G half moves rho, because E cancels out of it.
# With E = 16000 the set survives any r >= 1.2*E/G = 10.667, and T1's lower-bound form until
# the marginal reaches 1.2*E/G + 10*|slope| = 12.409 at read 6's slope (27.4 accepted nets
# out) or 11.737 at read 5's (50.8 nets).
#
# r_lo = 16.6532 is NOT the sampling formula r*(1-1/sqrt(n)) this file used for the 20-game
# probe. Over the 8000 games the marginal rests on, that formula's term is 1.12 % -- it
# bounds SAMPLING error, and sampling error is no longer the binding uncertainty. DRIFT
# is: r falls as the net trains. So r_lo is the marginal carried TEN accepted nets forward
# at the measured least-squares slope of rows/game over the last nine complete net
# directories, -0.1742 rows/game per accepted net at read 6, -0.1070 at read 5 (per_net in
# evidence/production_chain/throughput.json; reproduced by
# `check_knobs_9x9.py --marginal-nets 3 --trend-nets 9 --horizon-nets 10`). The newest net
# directory is excluded from every fit: it is the net still playing and its npz files lag
# its own sgfs, which is why the same 18035 rows read as 15.88 rows/game in the read-4 table
# (1136 games) and 17.34 in rows_per_game.txt (1040).
#
# WHY NOT MORE GAMES ALONE, AND WHY NOT A SMALLER EPOCH ALONE. Raising G is the only lever
# that moves rho, but at E = 20000 it must be re-derived at every read that sees the
# marginal fall (1800 at 17.72, 2100 at 15, 2500 at 13) -- the ratchet o47 asks to replace.
# Lowering E is free but, by the whole-file measurement above, restores only the WRITTEN
# tolerance: it changes neither samples/cycle nor rho. Option C takes both, so one move
# buys 27.4 accepted nets at read 6's steepened slope (50.8 at read 5's) and survives
# r = 13, where G alone or E alone would have to be moved again sooner.
# The full comparison, with every K1-K7/T1-T4 inequality at r = 17.7235, 15 and 13, is
# evidence/scale_data_window/o47_options.md.
NUM_GAMES_PER_CYCLE=1800

# Upstream :58, unchanged. Measured 4-thread parent + 8 single-thread workers = 12 threads.
NUM_THREADS_FOR_SHUFFLING=8

# 16000 = 125 * BATCHSIZE. LOWERED FROM 20000 ON 2026-09-05 (option C). E/batch = 125 >=
# 100 = print_train_loss_every_batches (train.py:1379), so metrics_train.json is written at
# all -- at the smoke's 38 batches/epoch it stayed 0 bytes. The nominal floor is
# 100*128 = 12800 and 16000 keeps 25 batches (20 %) of margin over it.
#
# What E does and does NOT do here (evidence/scale_data_window/epoch_granularity.txt):
# it does NOT set what an epoch trains -- one shuffled output file of ~60000 rows = 467
# batches does, because train.py:1306-1345 takes whole files -- so the metrics floor is
# currently unreachable and an epoch logs floor(467/100) = 4 lines whatever E is. What E
# DOES set is (a) the train-bucket consumption unit (train.py:1434-1438 spends E per epoch
# out of a bucket capped at max(MAX_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_CYCLE, E) at :1257), which is what
# the T1 freshness tolerance is written against; (b) swa_period_samples = E//2; and
# (c) num_batches_per_subepoch, a target the file granularity overshoots. Lowering E to
# 12800 was rejected: it would remove the metrics margin for no gain and would cut the
# export's SWA/EMA horizon (~4*E samples, train.py:441 with swa_scale 8 at :443) from 80000
# to 51200, i.e. less regularisation on the value head that read 5 measured drifting up.
# Upstream :59 = 100000 (a 19x19 number).
NUM_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_EPOCH=16000

# Upstream :60, the hard reuse cap; never raised (task file section 13). The bucket-side
# reuse of this set is 2.51 (lower90 2.67) and the reuse the loop really applies is
# rho = SHUFFLE_KEEPROWS/(G*r) = 3.88 at the read-6 marginal, so a 2x error in r still
# cannot cross 8. [WATCH] link 1, still at G = 1000, runs at rho = 6.99 = 87 % of this cap,
# and 7.78 at its 10-net bound; the R14 starvation signal has not fired (one "not enough new
# data rows" occurrence, longest run 1, threshold 3) but that is the closest any stage of
# this run has come to the cap. Link 2 at G = 1800 roughly halves it.
MAX_TRAIN_PER_DATA=8

# = NUM_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_EPOCH // 2, which is train.py:441's own default made explicit.
# Moves with E (10000 -> 8000 on 2026-09-05). With swa_scale 8 (train.py:443) the exported
# net is an EMA over ~8 * swa_period = 4*E = 64000 samples, down from 80000.
NUM_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_SWA=8000

# Upstream :62, unchanged. Peak VRAM was 4094 MiB at batch 32 on a 180 GB B200, so 128
# has ~40x headroom; and 125 batches/epoch is what the metrics floor above needs.
BATCHSIZE=128

# In cycle 1 the shuffle window IS min_rows (shuffle.py:1077 caps the usable random rows
# there, and :414-435 then returns exactly min_rows), so min_rows alone decides whether
# cycle 1 can fill an epoch: train.py:1303-1346 returns None -- and -quit-if-no-data exits 0
# with NO export (:1487-1488) -- below round(E/batch) batches. The binding form is
# min_rows/batch >= round(E/batch): 25000 rows = 195 batches against the 125 an epoch now
# needs, ratio 1.56 (it was 1.25 at E = 20000). Reachable: 1800 * r0_lo = 55851 >= 25000 at
# the production random-net r0_lo (31.028 over 5000 games; the smoke's 28.134 gave 42201).
# HELD AT 25000 by both 2026-09-05 re-derivations rather than tracked to 1.25*E = 20000:
# lowering it would only shrink a cycle-1 bootstrap margin that nothing else needs, and it
# is not even consulted by chain link 2, which resumes a mature window pinned at
# SHUFFLE_KEEPROWS = 120000 rows, 4.8 times min_rows. Upstream :63 = 100000.
SHUFFLE_MINROWS=25000

# = 5 * E, keeping upstream's own cap/epoch ratio of 5 (:64 500000 / :59 100000); moves with
# E (100000 -> 80000 on 2026-09-05). This knob is what fixes EPOCHS_PER_EXPORT, because
# train.py:1257 caps the bucket at max(MAX_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_CYCLE, E) and :1434 spends E
# per epoch. The ratio survives the measurement: 5*E = 80000 <= (MAX_TRAIN_PER_DATA/2) *
# G * r_lo = 111109, i.e. even at half the reuse cap and at the conservative r the cycle's
# own new data pays for all five epochs. NOTE (epoch_granularity.txt): the bucket is not
# what actually limits a cycle -- the two shuffled output files do, at 2 completed epochs --
# so this cap is a ceiling with slack, not the operating point.
MAX_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_CYCLE=80000

# Upstream :65, unchanged: no measured constraint binds it. It only sets where the shuffle
# window's growth turns sublinear (shuffle.py:421); at 9x9 scale the window is still in its
# near-linear regime (window ~ min_rows + 0.4 * post-random rows) for the first ~20 cycles.
TAPER_WINDOW_SCALE=50000

# HELD AT 120000 by the 2026-09-05 option-C move, so it is now 1.5 * MAX_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_
# CYCLE rather than the 1.2 of upstream's keep/cap ratio (:66 600000 / :64 500000). It still
# satisfies the shipped rule keep > cap (:66) and holds a whole cycle's draw
# (5*E = 80000 rows) with 50 % margin. It was NOT scaled down with the cap because
# evidence/scale_data_window/epoch_granularity.txt measures that this knob, not the cap, is
# what a cycle actually trains: samples/cycle = SHUFFLE_KEEPROWS = 120000, one pass over the
# kept sample in round(120000/70000) = 2 output files (shuffle.sh:48, shuffle.py:406-412).
# Lowering it would cut training volume per cycle and, below ~98000, collapse the sample to
# ONE output file and halve the export cadence. Upstream :66 = 600000.
SHUFFLE_KEEPROWS=120000

# = floor(min(gain, cap_eff) / E) with gain = G*r*MAX_TRAIN_PER_DATA = 255218 (lower90
# 239806) and cap_eff = max(cap, E) = 80000 -> 5, the same at r and at r_lo.
# Passed to train.py as BOTH -epochs-per-export and -max-epochs-this-instance, so the
# persistent export_cycle_counter (train.py:871,975,1743,1831) can advance by at most 5 per
# cycle: never more than ONE candidate per cycle, from cycle 1 on.
# THE RAMP (obligation o40, corrected 2026-09-04): while models/ is empty every cycle plays
# the random net and shuffle.py:1077 caps its usable rows at SHUFFLE_MINROWS, so the window
# is pinned at 25000 rows = ONE epoch per cycle (-no-repeat-files ends the instance when the
# shuffled files run out, training_data_generator.py:35; -quit-if-no-data then exits 0 with
# no export, train.py:1487-1489). The persistent counter therefore needs five such cycles:
# the FIRST candidate exports at cycle 5 and is gated at cycle 6, real-net rows exist only
# from the cycle whose gate ACCEPTS, and exactly one export per cycle begins at cycle 16
# under acceptance at 6 (later per rejection). No knob moves: every K1-K7/T1-T4 inequality is
# evaluated at cycle 1's window, which a random-net cycle never goes below.
# Re-derive the table with codes/eval/derive_knobs.py (EXPORT RAMP BY CYCLE).
# IN STEADY STATE the counter advances by TWO per cycle, not five: the shuffled sample is
# two output files and an epoch consumes one, so a candidate is exported every
# EPOCHS_PER_EXPORT/2 = 2.5 cycles (measured 22 exports in 62 cycles = 2.82 with the ramp).
# evidence/scale_data_window/epoch_granularity.txt. This knob and SHUFFLE_KEEPROWS are the
# only two that set that cadence; option C moved neither.
EPOCHS_PER_EXPORT=5

# --- the two allocation-side knobs, coupled to the ones above --------------------
# These are NOT read by synchronous_loop_9x9.sh. They are the numbers that
# codes/loop/loop.sbatch (#SBATCH --cpus-per-task, REQ_CPUS) and codes/cfg/{selfplay,
# gatekeeper}_9x9.cfg (numGameThreads) must carry; wiring them is o38, owned by
# loop_resume_under_walltime / cfg_9x9_override, not by this node.
#
# DESIGN section 1's [BLOCKING]: a 24-CPU declaration is false for any stage holding a CUDA
# context -- job 298712 measured nlwp_max 25 on both real-net stages against the 22 that
# every random-net selfplay measured, the +3 being CUDA runtime threads that
# debugSkipNeuralNet never creates (setup.cpp:126). Two repairs were admissible; with the
# CPU cap withdrawn (mission.json decisions[0]) raising the declaration is free, while
# cutting numGameThreads would lower the NN queue depth that DESIGN section 1 already calls
# the reason the GPU sits at 21.5 % mean duty. So the game threads stay and the declaration
# rises.
#
# Thread budget at numGameThreads 18, worst stage first:
#   gatekeeper, two real nets  18 + 2 nnServer + 1 dataWrite + 1 main + 2x3 CUDA = 28
#   selfplay, real net + switch 18 + 1 nnServer + 1 dataWrite + 1 modelLoad + 1 main
#                               + 3 CUDA = 25 (measured), + 2 net-switch transient = 27
#   train                       14 (measured, OMP_NUM_THREADS=MKL_NUM_THREADS=4)
#   shuffle                     4-thread parent + 8 worker processes = 12 (measured)
# max = 28 <= 32, headroom 4. (The 28 is the sum of the terms on the gatekeeper line; the 29
# printed here before 2026-09-04 mis-added them -- obligation o40. Nothing downstream moves:
# the declaration was already 32 and T4 asserts worst <= 32.) One node has 124 CPUs and there
# is no CPU cap; the rule that survives is only "declared >= used".
KTG_CPUS_PER_TASK=32
KTG_NUM_GAME_THREADS=18

# --- projections that come with this set (not knobs; recorded so they can be refuted) ---
# MEASURED over 65 production cycles, job 301099 on l40s node gl111
# (evidence/production_chain/throughput.json at monitoring read 6), superseding both the
# smoke projections and the read-4/5 figures this block carried before:
#   real-net selfplay 6429.7 games/h  (60904 real-net games over the 61 cycle selfplay
#                     phases from cycle 6, the first gate, on; 34100.39 s). The file's
#                     all-net 6871.9 credits the 5 random-bootstrap cycles, which ran
#                     1000 games in ~85 s each, and is NOT the rate link 2 gets.
#   train             2119.650 samples/s   (6857216 samples / 3235.07 s)
#   gate              0.48678 s per gate game (1541.13 s / 3166 gate games)
#   bytes/row         350.92 B on disk
# cycle wall  = 1008 s selfplay (1800 games at 6429.7 games/h, NO derate: the 0.5x was an
#                     allowance for a 20-game probe, and this is 61 production cycles)
#             +   38 s train (5*16000 samples at 2119.7 samples/s)
#             +  195 s gate + 84 s shuffle + 60 s export = 1384 s = 0.385 h
#             -> 185 whole cycles inside the DECLARED 257400 s, and 61 inside the GRANTED
#                84600 s TimeLimit of link 2 (job 305318). [PRELIMINARY]
#                The DIRECTLY measured production cycle is 0.1758 h at 1000 games; this
#                model is the more pessimistic of the two because its gate term charges
#                every cycle for a two-net gate at 200 games, where production gates every
#                ~2.8 cycles at ~141 games. The measured-basis extrapolation to this set is
#                3600*1800/6429.7 + 120000/2119.65 + 109.8 = 1174 s = 0.326 h, i.e. 219
#                cycles per declared link and 72 per granted link; the truth is between the
#                two and the first link-2 read will measure it. The train term uses
#                SHUFFLE_KEEPROWS, not epochs*E, for the reason in the
#                NUM_GAMES_PER_CYCLE block.
# storage     = 17.12 MiB/cycle monotonic (tdata 10.34 + sgfs 3.52 + gate sgfs 0.39
#               + one export 2.87)
#             + 551.9 MiB bounded steady state (3 shuffle dirs, 10 rejected models,
#               10 checkpoints, 3 dated archives)
#             -> 23.63 GiB after a full 185-cycle link against the 500 GiB cap
#                (KTG_SCRATCH_HARD_BYTES in codes/data_budget/budget.env); 28682 cycles
#                would fit before the cap.
#
# [OPEN] the storage model UNDER-counts: production grew runs/p1 to 1992576181 B in 65
#        cycles = 29.2 MiB/cycle, 1.7x this model (the absolute figure fell between reads 5
#        and 6 because the bounded classes are pruned). T3 holds by three orders of
#        magnitude either way; the gap is a defect of the model, owned by
#        data_budget / measure_stage_throughput.
# [OPEN] the rows/game drift has NOT stopped -- see the NUM_GAMES_PER_CYCLE block and
#        obligation o48_rows_per_game_drift_rederive_at_13.
# [CLOSED 2026-09-05] real-net games/hour and train samples/s, which used to rest on 100
#        probe games and one probe train: 6429.7 games/h and 2119.650 samples/s over 65
#        production cycles.
# [CLOSED 2026-09-05] the two-real-net gatekeeper's thread count, which used to be
#        arithmetic (28) on a one-net measurement of 25: production measured nlwp_max 24
#        on the gatekeeper over 15 gate stages, 24 selfplay, 13 train, 4 shuffle, all
#        ppid-filtered, against the declared 32. KTG_CPUS_PER_TASK 32 has headroom 8.
# WIRED 2026-09-04 (o39): codes/loop/loop.sbatch declares --cpus-per-task=32, reads REQ_CPUS
#        from KTG_CPUS_PER_TASK in this file, and refuses to start a link whose granted
#        SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK or whose cfg numGameThreads disagrees with these two values.
#
# --- what this set OVERTURNS, and what re-arms next -------------------------------
# APPLIED 2026-09-05 (human decision, mission.json decisions: "link 2 onward runs OPTION
# C"). This file supersedes the admitted 1500 / 20000 / 10000 / 100000 set: any statement
# resting on rows_per_cycle = 26585, on 5*E = 100000 samples per cycle, or on
# EPOCHS_PER_EXPORT reading five epochs of 20000, is stale. The RUNNING link (job 301099)
# is unaffected -- it logged its knob sha256 at start and a running link cannot adopt a new
# file -- so the change reaches production at link 2 (job 305318), which sources this file
# at link start via codes/loop/loop.sbatch.
# o47_rows_per_game_drift_rederive_at_17 is discharged by this move; the successor is
# o48_rows_per_game_drift_rederive_at_13, which re-arms at marginal r = 13.0 -- above the
# 12.409 at which this set's own 10-net drift bound stops clearing 1.2*E/G = 10.667, so the
# trigger fires with margin rather than at the failure. At the read-6 slope that is 24.0
# accepted nets away (39.0 at read 5's). THE SLOPE ITSELF IS NOT STABLE: it went -0.1070 ->
# -0.1742 between reads 5 and 6, so o48 requires the SLOPE to be recomputed at every
# monitoring read, not only the marginal.
